WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
- One of the things
we saw in 2020

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:05.171 align:start position:0%
was that it's actually
unsustainable to live that way,

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.108
you actually need
like a final truth

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:11.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
to add a vertical dimension
to your existence.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:15.215
And the need for that is
why people knelt so readily

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:16.983
for the BLM people, right?

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:19.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was they've needed
something to kneel before,

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:22.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
or like, for instance, the
Fauci of it all, right?

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.224
"I represent the science."

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:26.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's obviously not talking
there as a scientist,

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:28.495 align:start size:84% position:16%
but he is fulfilling
a very deep need

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that is for
clerical authority.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.599 align:start size:81% position:19%
So the proposition
in the book is,

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.101 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Look, you're going to
worship something actually,

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.770
there's going to be a bedrock."

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.272
So why would you get
these putzes like Fauci

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:40.774
to serve in that role?

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:43.576 align:start position:0%
Why wouldn't you seek, you know,
a deeper and a more ancient

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.246 align:start size:88% position:13%
and a more tried and
trusted version of that.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:48.815
(upbeat music)

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.891 align:start size:88% position:13%
is the host of the
"Young Heretics" podcast

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.893
and author of the new book,

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.962
I've got it right here in my
hand, "How to Save the West:

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:06.499
Ancient Wisdom for
Five Modern Crises."

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:09.002 align:start size:97% position:3%
Spencer Klavan, welcome
back to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:11.771
- Dave, it's a pleasure to be
here. Thanks for having me.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.874
- Spencer, I believe this
is your first solo time

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.409
on "The Rubin Report."

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.079
You've been a panel guest,
but as a published author

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.648 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a hard cover
book, and might I say,

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.850 align:start size:97% position:3%
on the back with the blurbs,
you really killed it.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.520
One of the names on this
list is just spectacular.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
This Megan Kelly. Oh,
Dave Rubin's on there too,

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:33.359
that's very nice.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:34.527
- Well, there he is.

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:35.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
I didn't think
anybody respectable

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.963
would want to be
associated with me

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.099 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so that's why I
reached out to you. No.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.170
- I do what I can
to help the sales.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.573
I'm glad to have you on solo.

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:49.142
Truth be told, we are
also friends outside of

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:53.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
these little internet boxes
and we often talk about

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.782 align:start size:84% position:16%
basically what
your book is about,

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:58.518 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it really is about
what so many of the things

00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:00.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I do on a daily basis
on this show are about.

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:04.090
So that will set us up for
this thing. So first off,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.960
for the people that just maybe
haven't seen you on the show,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.394
aren't familiar with you,

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.363
give me a little
of the background

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:11.798 align:start size:88% position:13%
that leads you to
writing a book like this

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.066
because you've got
quite a pedigree

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.301 align:start size:88% position:13%
that this does make
sense for you to write.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.236
- Oh, sure.

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:18.505
Well I, as you know, you know,

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.707
I went to all the fancy schools,

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:24.144
which is kind of the
like top line reason

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.079
why I know about the, you know,

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:28.248
the great text of the ancients.

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:29.883
But I think, you know,

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:32.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
somebody who comes out of
the universities these days,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.588
especially in America, has
a lot of explaining to do

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:38.458 align:start size:88% position:13%
because the schools
are just falling apart.

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:41.628
And I think that, you know,

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:45.865 align:start size:97% position:3%
basically what I wanted to
suggest to people in this book

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:49.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
and throughout my
whole career is that

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:52.005
you look at the news
cycle and every day,

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:54.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
it seems like there's
some new crisis

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is flashing
across the headlines.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
Something else is going wrong,
they're transing the kids

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
all of a sudden, the
economy is falling apart.

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:03.950
You know, the internet seems
to be destroying everything

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.920
and like America
is breaking apart.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.988
And what I wanted to propose,

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:12.625 align:start size:97% position:3%
and this is based on a
lifelong love of the classics

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.994
and of the great
texts of the West,

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:18.565 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that even though
every day seems to bring

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:21.367
kind of something
new to fret about,

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.771
a lot of the questions that
are underlying our problems

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:27.106 align:start size:97% position:3%
are questions that have
been asked again and again.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.642
In fact, they go back
to the origins really

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:33.846
of human thought and some of
the answers that come down

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:37.283
to us from antiquity
are better and saner

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:40.253
than the stuff you get
out of your modern gurus.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:43.523
So even as the academy
is kind of going insane,

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:46.859 align:start size:97% position:3%
and even as I was traveling
through the academy,

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:50.430
and seeing some of that madness,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.500 align:start size:97% position:3%
at the same time, I was really
confirmed in my conviction

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.437
that these great books which
sort of sit on the shelves,

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:58.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
they look kind of
dusty, intimidating,

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.139
they're actually for you.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.675
They're about how to
be good at being human.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.678 align:start size:84% position:16%
And these are, you
know, points of wisdom

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:08.147
that we particularly need
now as the digital era

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:12.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of rockets
into it's new phase.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:15.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
So effectively what I'm doing
in this book is I'm just,

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, inviting people
to consider the fact

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.426 align:start size:84% position:16%
that these aren't
actually new problems,

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:25.431
they're taking new forms, but
there are precedents for them.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:27.533
There's a way to go
back into the past

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:29.102
and talk sanely about them,

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:31.738
not just for kooky college
professors with blue hair,

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.240
not just for nerds like me
who go to fancy schools,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.609
but for everybody and
especially for people

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:40.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
that care about this
country and want to save it.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.681
- Spencer, if I'm
understanding you,

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:43.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're telling me that
there is knowledge

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:47.186
that people before us knew
and that there are things

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:51.024
and ways of analyzing
reality that luminaries

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:55.762
such as, I don't know, CNN's
Don Lemon or MSNBC's Joy Reid,

00:04:55.762 --> 00:05:00.700
perhaps don't have a great
ability to tell us about?

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:02.568
- I mean, I don't want to sound-

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.836
- I thought they
were also smart.

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:05.305
I thought the whole
point is that people

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:07.473 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the modern day are so
smart and everyone before us

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:10.243
was a bunch of racist
backwards homophobes.

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.345 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And misogynists,
don't forget about that.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
So yeah, I mean, I'm
definitely like, you know,

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:18.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
every possible slur
you could throw at me,

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:21.788
I accept them all if
what that means is just

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.290
that I don't think
wisdom or information

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.693
was invented in 1965.

00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:28.094
I mean, in the book,

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:29.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
I call this chronological
chauvinism, right?

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:31.798 align:start size:81% position:19%
The idea that like
newer is better

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:36.169 align:start position:0%
and basically every answer about
man's place in the universe

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.639 align:start size:97% position:3%
was discovered
post-scientific revolution.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:41.541
Yeah, those guys you mentioned,

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:44.043 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Don Lemons of
the world, you know,

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:47.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Nancy Pelosi of the world,
Edward Saids of the world,

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.384
they'll smear at you for
suggesting that actually

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:54.487 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's not inherently evil
to talk about things

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.022
like western culture.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.258
And they laugh as if this is
like a ludicrous suggestion.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.527
But why shouldn't it
be the case that people

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:04.664 align:start size:97% position:3%
who have thought deeply
about these things over time

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:07.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
and throughout the
history of our traditions

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.569
have arrived at some
pretty good answers?

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:12.271 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think we're cheating
ourselves out of those answers

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:15.274 align:start size:88% position:13%
if we ignore or we just
cast off everything

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.610
that went before us to
some kind of like racist,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.479
chauvinist holdover.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:21.180 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right, and it's
weird because at least

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
at a mainstream level,
that seems to be

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:25.051
what people are doing
at every given moment.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:27.987 align:start size:97% position:3%
We just wake up, you see
what's going on in the world,

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:32.125 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you have some really sort
of half weighted dingbat,

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:34.961
I'm not as learned as you
are, so those are my words,

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:37.163
who are analyzing this stuff.

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:39.932
But I thought a nice way
of doing this interview

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:41.834 align:start size:84% position:16%
would be you basically
are laying out,

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:44.303
you've sort of already laid out

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:46.506
what the five sort of
topics of the book are,

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.474
the five crises that
we're dealing with,

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:49.709
but we could just
kind of go through,

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.844
spend a few minutes
on each one actually.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:53.813
So the first one
that you talk about

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:56.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
is the crisis of reality,
and this is sort of connected

00:06:56.916 --> 00:07:01.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
to our inability to
actually say what is true

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:04.791 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that people have their
truth, not the truth,

00:07:04.791 --> 00:07:07.093 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that this has also
gotten complicated

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:09.962
because of the machines
and AI and all that.

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:13.766
- Yeah. Well I mean, it's
funny when 2016 happened

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:17.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
and bad orange man was
elected to the presidency,

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:21.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
you suddenly had this
new concern about
post-truth politics

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:23.810 align:start size:84% position:16%
that all of a sudden
like, you know,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.712
that Trump is saying things
that aren't strictly true

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:29.115
and like he has hype
men who spin for him

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:31.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
and all of these
things. And you know,

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:35.855
even I am old enough
to remember that like

00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:40.526 align:start size:88% position:13%
the arguments over what
the meaning of ISIS

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:43.529 align:start size:84% position:16%
go back to before
Donald Trump, right?

00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:45.465
I mean, you had
when Clinton was-

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:46.899
- I think that was around '96.

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:48.468
- I vaguely recall, yes.

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:51.003 align:start position:0%
I think that was, you know,
and then you've got stuff like,

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.105
you know, the memos on Bush,

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:55.241
the killing memos on Bush
were fake but accurate.

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:57.910
That was a New York Times
headline about, you know,

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.346
these sort of falsified
charges against Bush

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:01.581
that Dan Rather raised.

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:03.950
And once you start
to look at this,

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
you realize this is not like
a bad orange man problem.

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:08.087 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is a much,
much deeper problem.

00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:10.723
And I think it goes
back to a question

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:12.925
that was actually raised
right at the very beginning

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:14.460
of Western philosophy.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.262 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's like you
said, it's what is true.

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:19.832
Is there such a thing
as true and false?

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:22.635
People think that they can
just answer that question

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:24.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the negative and sound
sophisticated, like,

00:08:24.804 --> 00:08:26.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh yeah, there's nothing
either good or bad,

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:28.574
but thinking makes it so."

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
And my argument is that
there's no halfway version

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:34.080
of that proposition.
If you take that pill,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.581
you got to go all the way.

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:37.750
And what that means is the
only thing there is is power,

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.252
which is what you're
seeing now, right?

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:41.554
People will just insist that
you call them by the pronouns

00:08:41.554 --> 00:08:44.624 align:start position:0%
that they have invented because
their reality, their truth,

00:08:44.624 --> 00:08:46.859
is what they're going to
enforce using the state,

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:48.728 align:start size:81% position:19%
if they can get
their hands on it.

00:08:48.728 --> 00:08:51.697 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's the first section
of the book is about

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:54.800
kind of the ancient answer
to that important question,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is yes, some
things are true or false,

00:08:57.236 --> 00:08:58.471
whether you wish them away,

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:01.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether you digitize
them away or not.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.976
And if you don't want to live

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.711 align:start size:81% position:19%
in a world of pure
power politics,

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:09.048 align:start size:97% position:3%
you've got to get on board
that train, the reality train.

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:10.716
- So what could we do now?

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:12.685 align:start size:81% position:19%
Because so much
of what, you know,

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:14.554
I do a daily show every day,

00:09:14.554 --> 00:09:18.391 align:start size:97% position:3%
I do a daily show every
day. It's quite remarkable.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:19.592
- Wow.

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:21.160
- Every day, I'm
doing a daily show.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:22.795
That is true, by
the way, Spencer.

00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:25.031 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You do it every day. Yeah.
- Absolutely true.

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:26.499
But what can we do?

00:09:26.499 --> 00:09:28.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I'm always talking
about how we're having

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:30.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
this sort of breakdown
in reality because

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.371
if you watch certain things,

00:09:32.371 --> 00:09:35.441
what is true in your
world is very different

00:09:35.441 --> 00:09:38.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
than perhaps what
is true in my world.

00:09:38.344 --> 00:09:39.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- I don't sit here telling you

00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:40.880
that I own the truth,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.318 align:start position:0%
but how do we sort of
arbitrage those two ideas that,

00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:48.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, this is
this and that is that,

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:50.823
we're going to start living
in very different places.

00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:53.192
- But sure enough, I
mean, I would say that

00:09:53.192 --> 00:09:55.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
the question I continually
encourage people

00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:58.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
to ask themselves and
others throughout the book

00:09:58.164 --> 00:10:00.566 align:start size:84% position:16%
is how's that
working out for you?

00:10:00.566 --> 00:10:03.636
And I think like
in the abstract,

00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:06.272 align:start size:97% position:3%
you can sit in a
philosophy seminar and say,

00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:08.240
"Well, my truth is just
as good as your truth

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.543
or you need to accept my truth."

00:10:10.543 --> 00:10:13.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in point of fact,
when you start to do that,

00:10:13.813 --> 00:10:15.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
you notice all these
ways that it breaks down,

00:10:15.915 --> 00:10:18.784 align:start size:88% position:13%
it broke down Stalinist
Russia, you know,

00:10:18.784 --> 00:10:21.454
when the professor
started arguing that

00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:23.823
there was no real need for
evidence to convict somebody

00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:26.359
because we just know, like
according to spiritually,

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:29.061 align:start size:88% position:13%
according to the party,
that he's guilty.

00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:33.265 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you see all the disastrous
consequences of this stuff

00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:34.567
in the here and now.

00:10:34.567 --> 00:10:36.202
And the more we can
bring each other

00:10:36.202 --> 00:10:37.503
back to the here and now,

00:10:37.503 --> 00:10:39.405
the more we can engage
in like face-to-face.

00:10:39.405 --> 00:10:42.041
I mean, I know here
we are on a digital,

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:43.109
like communication system,

00:10:43.109 --> 00:10:44.677
but you mentioned at the outset

00:10:44.677 --> 00:10:46.412
like we have a real
in-person friendship.

00:10:46.412 --> 00:10:49.315
We share ideas with one another

00:10:49.315 --> 00:10:51.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
and people are
really craving that.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.253
They've been told that
they like don't need it

00:10:54.253 --> 00:10:57.323 align:start size:97% position:3%
or that Zoom is fine, lockdown
is good for you actually,

00:10:57.323 --> 00:10:59.492
you'll know nothing, you'll
live in the pod, whatever.

00:10:59.492 --> 00:11:01.027
We know that doesn't work.

00:11:01.027 --> 00:11:04.430
So the thing I insist on is
like a constant face-to-face

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:07.500 align:start size:97% position:3%
encounter with one another,
especially in our communities.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:08.801
That's why the stuff
that's going on in

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:10.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
like the school
boards are so exciting

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
because that's where people
actually have to like,

00:11:12.838 --> 00:11:16.342 align:start size:97% position:3%
face up to the consequences
of their beliefs

00:11:16.342 --> 00:11:17.276
and of their actions.

00:11:17.276 --> 00:11:18.644
And the more we insist on that,

00:11:18.644 --> 00:11:21.847
I think the closer we
get back to reality.

00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:26.352
- All right, so if we slowly
but surely get back to reality,

00:11:26.352 --> 00:11:28.654
that would be a nice concept,

00:11:28.654 --> 00:11:30.423
that would sort of get us
to the second part of this

00:11:30.423 --> 00:11:34.126
because you talk about
the crisis of the body.

00:11:34.126 --> 00:11:36.662
And right now, we seem
to be in this situation

00:11:36.662 --> 00:11:41.667
where many people's reality
of their physical reality

00:11:43.169 --> 00:11:45.805
seemingly is not matching
up with their mental model

00:11:45.805 --> 00:11:47.506
or their mental reality.

00:11:47.506 --> 00:11:50.342
And the rest of us are
constantly talking about

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:52.745 align:start size:84% position:16%
this trans issue,
despite the fact that

00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:55.881
it's very small percentage wise,

00:11:55.881 --> 00:11:59.585
it seems to have leaked into
like every part of society.

00:11:59.585 --> 00:12:01.487
- That's right. And
you make a really,

00:12:01.487 --> 00:12:03.589
I think that's a great point
that we kind of fixate on it

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:06.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
even if we aren't
feeling it ourselves.

00:12:06.258 --> 00:12:08.928
And that's kind of a key,
I think, to a lot of this

00:12:08.928 --> 00:12:11.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
because when this kind
of stuff comes up,

00:12:11.697 --> 00:12:14.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
of course it's really
easy just to get into like

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:17.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
a kind of conservative
fist pounding mode of like

00:12:17.036 --> 00:12:20.606
those bad deviants, right?
They're doing the bad stuff.

00:12:20.606 --> 00:12:24.276
And one of the things I try
to point out in the book is,

00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:27.379 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, this is
definitely a dangerous

00:12:27.379 --> 00:12:31.183
and destructive
kind of ideology,

00:12:31.183 --> 00:12:34.220
the notion that you can like
pump children full of hormones

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:37.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
is obviously I think
evil and wrong.

00:12:37.356 --> 00:12:39.024
But in order to understand it,

00:12:39.024 --> 00:12:42.695
if we really want to kind of
get over it or move past it,

00:12:42.695 --> 00:12:44.597
we have to understand
that it's a response

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:47.433 align:start size:88% position:13%
to a very, very ancient
and deep problem,

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:49.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is the
problem of the body,

00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:50.503
the problem of the flesh.

00:12:50.503 --> 00:12:52.972
Which like, you know,
you can find people

00:12:52.972 --> 00:12:56.776
from the early days of Greek
philosophy wrestling with this

00:12:56.776 --> 00:13:00.146
in the book I talk about,
a neo-Platonist philosopher

00:13:00.146 --> 00:13:03.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
called Plotinus whose
biographer from antiquity says

00:13:03.616 --> 00:13:06.886
he seemed ashamed of
being in his body.

00:13:06.886 --> 00:13:09.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that the reason
these two things are connected

00:13:09.588 --> 00:13:11.457
is because when you start
talking about reality,

00:13:11.457 --> 00:13:12.992
about absolute truth,

00:13:12.992 --> 00:13:14.727
you very quickly start to
talk about ideals, right?

00:13:14.727 --> 00:13:18.898
Like beauty, things that are
the way they are regardless of,

00:13:18.898 --> 00:13:21.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, whoever
disagrees, you know,

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:24.904
absolute truth kind of lives
in this pure eternal sphere.

00:13:24.904 --> 00:13:27.072
And we don't live in
that sphere, we live in

00:13:27.072 --> 00:13:31.343
a really messy kind of world
where people break down and die

00:13:31.343 --> 00:13:34.180
and like, you know,
you get man colds

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:36.482
and all sorts of stuff, all
sorts of bad stuff happens.

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:39.518
And when you face
up against that,

00:13:39.518 --> 00:13:42.721
you start to want to kind
of crawl out of your flesh.

00:13:42.721 --> 00:13:45.090
And I think that
the trans ideology,

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.126
the transgender stuff
kind of plays on that,

00:13:47.126 --> 00:13:49.328
sort of offers to people
this vision of themselves

00:13:49.328 --> 00:13:53.732
that doesn't depend on, you
know, living in the flesh

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:56.702
on like seeking health and
goodness in the here and now,

00:13:56.702 --> 00:13:59.104
but promises some kind
of otherworldly future

00:13:59.104 --> 00:14:01.807
you can zoom up into. And
you start to see that this

00:14:01.807 --> 00:14:03.943
develops very quickly
out of transgenderism

00:14:03.943 --> 00:14:07.847
and into transhumanism into
like a whole new kind of,

00:14:07.847 --> 00:14:10.082 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're going to evaporate
into like some digital space,

00:14:10.082 --> 00:14:12.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're going to come become
an Android or whatever.

00:14:12.885 --> 00:14:15.054
Once again in the book, I
just insist on asking like,

00:14:15.054 --> 00:14:16.455
how is that working out for us?

00:14:16.455 --> 00:14:18.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, it's a
proposition that's based on

00:14:18.791 --> 00:14:21.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
something that's
currently making us
sick and and miserable.

00:14:21.894 --> 00:14:24.830
And the proposition is
like just maximize it,

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.398
just go to infinity.

00:14:26.398 --> 00:14:29.668
So I present in the book
kind of other way basically

00:14:29.668 --> 00:14:32.571
of looking at this that yes,
there's more to us than flesh,

00:14:32.571 --> 00:14:34.940
but the things that we
seek are actually found

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:36.575
through the flesh that
our bodies are kind of

00:14:36.575 --> 00:14:38.844
a language for our soul.

00:14:38.844 --> 00:14:42.581
And I think again, this
is like a much healthier

00:14:42.581 --> 00:14:45.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
and saner way to
engage with yourself

00:14:45.384 --> 00:14:47.319
and with the rest of the world,

00:14:47.319 --> 00:14:49.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it does require
that you actually like

00:14:49.521 --> 00:14:52.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
face up to the consequences
of your actions,

00:14:52.791 --> 00:14:54.760 align:start size:97% position:3%
which again requires
coming back to that reality

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:57.496
and actually like looking at
yourself and those around you

00:14:57.496 --> 00:15:00.266
and making a sober assessment.

00:15:00.266 --> 00:15:01.767 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, it's interesting
because we could spend

00:15:01.767 --> 00:15:05.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
all day long talking about
the trans part of this,

00:15:05.437 --> 00:15:07.273
but I think actually
maybe for a moment,

00:15:07.273 --> 00:15:10.342 align:start size:88% position:13%
we could spend on the
transhuman part of this.

00:15:10.342 --> 00:15:13.078 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is the idea that people
will replace body parts

00:15:13.078 --> 00:15:15.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
with robotic body
parts and, you know,

00:15:15.281 --> 00:15:19.184
digital IDs in your hand
and if your eyesight's

00:15:19.184 --> 00:15:21.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
not working, you could
potentially get, you know,

00:15:21.453 --> 00:15:23.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
some sort of robotic eye,
the litany of things,

00:15:23.622 --> 00:15:25.925 align:start size:84% position:16%
artificial wounds
outside of the body.

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:28.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's so many things
here, and for me,

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.430
I'm sort of two-minded on
this because on one hand,

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:34.033 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's this sci-fi
part of me that I grew,

00:15:34.033 --> 00:15:35.267
every movie that you,

00:15:35.267 --> 00:15:37.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you went through
my favorite 100 movies

00:15:37.303 --> 00:15:40.706
or most watched movies, 99
of them are probably sci-fi,

00:15:40.706 --> 00:15:42.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I have a great affinity
to all of this stuff

00:15:42.775 --> 00:15:44.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the imagination
of all of it.

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:46.612
And then there's this other
part where it's like, man,

00:15:46.612 --> 00:15:49.982 align:start position:0%
look what in 20 years the
internet has done to all of us,

00:15:49.982 --> 00:15:52.885
did it make us more social,
more happy, more connected,

00:15:52.885 --> 00:15:54.954 align:start size:81% position:19%
or did it actually
do the reverse?

00:15:54.954 --> 00:15:57.723
And I would now fear that
while it might be cool

00:15:57.723 --> 00:16:00.125
at some level to have
a super strong arm

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:02.661
like Will Smith had in "iRobot,"

00:16:02.661 --> 00:16:05.831 align:start position:0%
we might be trading on
something that we would regret,

00:16:05.831 --> 00:16:07.499 align:start size:81% position:19%
which actually
isn't that far off

00:16:07.499 --> 00:16:09.902
from the original
trans conversation.

00:16:09.902 --> 00:16:11.236 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, you
replaced some parts,

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:13.672
but there still might
be a problem there.

00:16:13.672 --> 00:16:16.175
- It's sort of like you
got promised Robocop,

00:16:16.175 --> 00:16:18.210 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they actually turned
you into the Borg.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:19.278
Do you know what I mean?

00:16:19.278 --> 00:16:20.212 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like there's like-
- Yes.

00:16:22.481 --> 00:16:23.949
- That's what you're describing.

00:16:23.949 --> 00:16:26.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
I feel it too because I
really don't want to become

00:16:26.085 --> 00:16:27.419 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of these guys
that's just saying like,

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:29.755
all this tech is bad,
we got to reverse it,

00:16:29.755 --> 00:16:31.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
we got to undo it.
That's always the danger.

00:16:31.857 --> 00:16:33.492
And I talk about this a
little bit in the opening

00:16:33.492 --> 00:16:36.428
of the book just because
it's very easy to slip into

00:16:36.428 --> 00:16:37.830
and to be accused
of like wanting

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.932
to turn back the
tide of history.

00:16:39.932 --> 00:16:42.801 align:start size:97% position:3%
Some of this stuff is going
to come into existence.

00:16:42.801 --> 00:16:45.537
There's no real version of
the future, I don't think,

00:16:46.972 --> 00:16:49.241
we don't start to do things
that, you know, for instance,

00:16:49.241 --> 00:16:51.176
like we're already
seeing these implants

00:16:51.176 --> 00:16:53.545 align:start size:84% position:16%
that can fix or
help with blindness,

00:16:53.545 --> 00:16:55.948
with people with hard hearing,

00:16:55.948 --> 00:16:58.517 align:start position:0%
and we can recognize that
there's something good in that.

00:16:58.517 --> 00:17:00.285
At the same time, like you say,

00:17:00.285 --> 00:17:02.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's also this
philosophy behind it.

00:17:02.955 --> 00:17:04.456
That's kind of what I'm
trying to get at here.

00:17:04.456 --> 00:17:08.594 align:start size:84% position:16%
This driving idea that
not only is it good

00:17:08.594 --> 00:17:12.231
to restore our faculties
or enhance our faculties,

00:17:12.231 --> 00:17:15.434
it's actually good
to just transcend

00:17:15.434 --> 00:17:17.536
what we are altogether.

00:17:17.536 --> 00:17:21.006
And I think that's why the
Borg is such a powerful image

00:17:21.006 --> 00:17:23.742 align:start position:0%
in "Star Trek" actually is
because that's about dissolving

00:17:23.742 --> 00:17:25.511
the boundaries of yourself.

00:17:25.511 --> 00:17:29.548
And the philosophy that
I resort to in the book

00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:31.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
to deal with this stuff,
which is called hylomorphism,

00:17:31.884 --> 00:17:35.187
a fancy word for form in
matter that you kind of,

00:17:35.187 --> 00:17:39.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
you never really get
beyond having a human body.

00:17:39.658 --> 00:17:42.461
You can, you know, use that
human body in different ways.

00:17:42.461 --> 00:17:45.697
I think that that's the
key to like deciding

00:17:45.697 --> 00:17:48.600
between the Robocop and
the Borg of it is like,

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
is this something that
kind of takes what we are,

00:17:51.937 --> 00:17:56.809
which is a soul in a body and
enhances it and, you know,

00:17:56.809 --> 00:18:01.747
brings us closer in some way
to the meaning of what we are

00:18:03.148 --> 00:18:04.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
or is it something that
alienates us from ourselves

00:18:04.683 --> 00:18:07.252
and ultimately kind of makes
us disgusted with our flesh.

00:18:07.252 --> 00:18:09.788
- Is the other part of this
where it's not even going to

00:18:09.788 --> 00:18:12.357
necessarily do that much
to your physical body,

00:18:12.357 --> 00:18:14.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you will have
followed Mark Zuckerberg,

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:17.062
who I'm fairly certain
is a synthetic android,

00:18:17.062 --> 00:18:19.698 align:start position:0%
into the metaverse, meaning
that you won't have to actually,

00:18:19.698 --> 00:18:21.900 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, the transhuman
part of this is that

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:23.769
you're literally going
to replace body parts,

00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:25.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
replace eyeballs,
things of that nature.

00:18:25.804 --> 00:18:27.139
But the other version of this is

00:18:27.139 --> 00:18:28.774
the purely digital
version of this,

00:18:28.774 --> 00:18:30.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
which would be more in
line with like "The Matrix"

00:18:30.976 --> 00:18:34.179
where we will still
exist like this,

00:18:34.179 --> 00:18:37.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we will be the
battery for the existence

00:18:37.182 --> 00:18:40.385 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the digital world
where you can be an ogre

00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:45.390 align:start position:0%
or a furry or whatever else
Beto O'Rourke is doing at night.

00:18:46.792 --> 00:18:49.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, well Beto, I'm
sure is already, you know,

00:18:49.728 --> 00:18:52.564
those guys already have the
more advanced technology

00:18:52.564 --> 00:18:54.066
we don't even have privy to,

00:18:54.066 --> 00:18:55.467
so I don't want to think
about like what's going on

00:18:55.467 --> 00:18:57.936
in that sphere. But
like no, I mean,

00:18:57.936 --> 00:18:59.571
this is the flip side of it.

00:18:59.571 --> 00:19:04.510 align:start position:0%
And rather than like fixing
your body or changing your body,

00:19:05.377 --> 00:19:06.612
the kind of offer here is,

00:19:06.612 --> 00:19:09.581 align:start size:88% position:13%
well, what if you can
just engineer your brain

00:19:09.581 --> 00:19:12.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
so that you feel things
that are totally unrelated

00:19:12.551 --> 00:19:13.852
to what's actually going on

00:19:13.852 --> 00:19:15.387
in your-
- Right.

00:19:15.387 --> 00:19:17.823
So literally in real life
you could be a 350 pound,

00:19:17.823 --> 00:19:22.828
5'6", you know, guy, and
in this digital universe,

00:19:23.996 --> 00:19:24.930
you could be the
hottest chick ever

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:26.765
or vice versa or whatever.

00:19:26.765 --> 00:19:28.000
- Exactly. Yeah.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.936 align:start size:88% position:13%
There are "Black Mirror"
episodes about this

00:19:30.936 --> 00:19:32.771
and you mentioned "The Matrix,"

00:19:32.771 --> 00:19:35.541 align:start position:0%
and actually I think that sci-fi
is a really interesting key

00:19:35.541 --> 00:19:37.075
here to thinking
about this stuff

00:19:37.075 --> 00:19:41.013 align:start position:0%
because there's really no sci-fi
version of this technology

00:19:41.013 --> 00:19:43.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
where it's just
totally a good thing

00:19:43.515 --> 00:19:47.352
and these are always kind of
presented in dystopian form.

00:19:47.352 --> 00:19:51.123 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's really easy to tell
this story about a dystopia.

00:19:51.123 --> 00:19:53.158 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's really hard to
tell a story about this

00:19:53.158 --> 00:19:54.660
that goes well.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:56.695
And one of the things I
argue throughout the book

00:19:56.695 --> 00:20:00.465
is that art, you know,
is kind of our inlet,

00:20:00.465 --> 00:20:03.535
our inroad to the intuitions
that we have kind of seated

00:20:03.535 --> 00:20:06.638
even deeper than what we can
really consciously articulate.

00:20:06.638 --> 00:20:08.407
And so there's something
important about the fact

00:20:08.407 --> 00:20:11.810 align:start size:97% position:3%
that all of these sci-fi
stories are dystopian stories.

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:13.045
And I think it's that, you know,

00:20:13.045 --> 00:20:14.913
whenever you pull
back the camera,

00:20:14.913 --> 00:20:17.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
whenever you tell that
third person story,

00:20:17.716 --> 00:20:20.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's always
somebody manipulating

00:20:20.352 --> 00:20:22.054
the virtual reality technology.

00:20:22.054 --> 00:20:25.524
That's why those press photos
of Zuck walking down the aisle

00:20:25.524 --> 00:20:28.360 align:start size:84% position:16%
with all those guys
with headsets strapped
to their faces.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.295
That's why those were
so damaging to him

00:20:30.295 --> 00:20:33.899
is because they kind of
reveal the third person

00:20:33.899 --> 00:20:36.635 align:start size:88% position:13%
objective view of
what's really going on.

00:20:36.635 --> 00:20:38.637
And I think we shouldn't
let ourselves be talked

00:20:38.637 --> 00:20:40.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
out of that intuition
that we have,

00:20:40.572 --> 00:20:43.008
that we express in our sci-fi.

00:20:43.008 --> 00:20:44.476
We ought to listen
to the Wachowskis

00:20:44.476 --> 00:20:45.811
because they were
onto something.

00:20:45.811 --> 00:20:47.212 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right. Well it's
just like, man,

00:20:47.212 --> 00:20:50.015
we all know this will lead us to

00:20:50.015 --> 00:20:51.783
something close to dystopia.

00:20:51.783 --> 00:20:54.953
Maybe we're in it already and
we don't even know that fully,

00:20:54.953 --> 00:20:57.089
but yet we seem to be
following the people

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:58.924 align:start size:84% position:16%
who screwed up so much
in the first place,

00:20:58.924 --> 00:21:00.225
Zuckerberg, for example.

00:21:00.225 --> 00:21:03.028
But let's move from that
to the crisis of meaning

00:21:03.028 --> 00:21:05.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
because obviously those
things are connected as well.

00:21:05.464 --> 00:21:08.700
There seems to be, at
least in a modern sense

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:13.038 align:start size:94% position:6%
amongst young people, a
complete crisis of meaning

00:21:13.038 --> 00:21:15.340
really just like
a sense of like,

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.943 align:start size:88% position:13%
what's the point of this
whole freaking thing?

00:21:17.943 --> 00:21:19.878
- Yep. Yeah, that's right.

00:21:19.878 --> 00:21:23.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think this kind of
boils down to, you know,

00:21:23.382 --> 00:21:26.718
you can see it in our
art and you can see it

00:21:26.718 --> 00:21:28.487
in our science at the same time,

00:21:28.487 --> 00:21:30.589
this kind of idea that like

00:21:30.589 --> 00:21:32.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're really just
throwing stuff at a wall,

00:21:32.924 --> 00:21:36.328 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, the
primordial soup is like

00:21:36.328 --> 00:21:38.397 align:start size:84% position:16%
generating all these
different options

00:21:38.397 --> 00:21:40.632
and some of them kind
of endure and survive

00:21:40.632 --> 00:21:41.800
and some of them don't.

00:21:43.001 --> 00:21:44.803
And I think that
this is, you know,

00:21:46.838 --> 00:21:48.540 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the rubber kind
of meets the road

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:51.910
on that relativism question,
that this stuff doesn't work

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:53.712
when you chase it
all the way down.

00:21:53.712 --> 00:21:55.080
And there's a simple reason why.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:57.716
It's like when we make
art, when we make memes,

00:21:57.716 --> 00:21:59.418
when we build civilizations,

00:21:59.418 --> 00:22:01.353
when we interact
with one another,

00:22:01.353 --> 00:22:04.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
we take it for granted
that there is some bedrock

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:08.093
to reality, some
kind of final good

00:22:08.093 --> 00:22:10.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
or ultimate existence
that, you know,

00:22:10.696 --> 00:22:13.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
everything else is kind
of referring back to that.

00:22:13.465 --> 00:22:16.935
And unless you have that, then
there's no like real meaning

00:22:16.935 --> 00:22:18.637
to your statements at all.

00:22:18.637 --> 00:22:21.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, we can never
really look directly on this,

00:22:21.173 --> 00:22:22.974
you know, this final truth,

00:22:22.974 --> 00:22:24.776
but we're always trying
to approximate to it.

00:22:24.776 --> 00:22:25.977
We're always trying
to represent it,

00:22:25.977 --> 00:22:28.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
express it in all
these different ways.

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:32.084
And the thesis of the like
